      "metagenics perfect protein whey",
      "metagenics ultrameal",
      "metagenics biopure protein",
      "is metagenics third-party tested"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Clean Protein Picks",
    link: "/best-clean-protein-powder/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Brand Review"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Metagenics Protein Powder Review</Fragment>
      Metagenics is the protein your chiropractor or functional-medicine doctor sells. Here is what is actually in Perfect Protein, BioPure and UltraMeal, what the brand's testing claims cover, and why the cost per gram runs two to six times that of ordinary whey.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#short-answer">The Short Answer</a>
      <a href="#third-party-tested">Is It Third-Party Tested?</a>
      <a href="#lineup">What Metagenics Sells Now</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">Which Products to Buy</a>
      <a href="#label">What Is in Perfect Protein Whey?</a>
      <a href="#medical-food">Medical Food: Do You Need UltraMeal?</a>
      <a href="#cost-per-25g">Cost per 25 g of Protein</a>
      <a href="#buyer-reviews">Taste, Mixing and Complaints</a>
      <a href="#vs-rivals">Metagenics vs Thorne and Retail Brands</a>
      <a href="#who-should-buy">Who Should Buy It</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Metagenics makes clean, carefully formulated protein powders that are hard to justify on price. Its best product, Perfect Protein Whey, gives 20 g of whey isolate protein in 100 calories per the label, at about $3.33 per 25 g. The brand says it tests for heavy metals and is in SuppCo's TESTED program, but none of its proteins is NSF Certified for Sport. Below: each product's label, the testing, the medical-food question and who should pay the premium.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Metagenics Protein Powder: The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Metagenics protein powder is good quality and poor value. The formulas are sensible, the ingredient lists are short, and the brand is more open about its quality systems than most. But you pay practitioner-channel prices: from about $3.12 per 25 g of protein for Perfect Protein Pea and Rice to more than $8 for the Essential Nutrition Shake, against roughly $1 to $1.50 for many mainstream wheys.
          </p>

          <p>
            On testing, the honest answer is partial. Metagenics says its products are tested for heavy metals, microbes and pesticides, that its facilities follow GMP, and that it issues batch certificates of analysis. It is also one of the launch brands in SuppCo's TESTED program, which buys products off the shelf and sends them to an independent lab. What it lacks is NSF Certified for Sport or a comparable athlete-focused seal on any protein we could find.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Perfect Protein Whey, per the label</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">20 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein per 27 g serving</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">100</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Calories</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">1 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Added sugar</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$3.33</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Per 25 g protein (Vitacost)</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Buy Metagenics if a practitioner you trust has recommended a specific product for a specific reason, or if you value its quality documentation enough to pay two to three times the going rate. Skip it if you just want a clean whey or plant protein, if you compete in a drug-tested sport, or if cost per gram matters to you. Comparable clean proteins cost far less.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="third-party-tested" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Is Metagenics Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Partly. Metagenics describes itself as third-party verified on its quality page, and a company FAQ says it voluntarily takes part in third-party testing. In March 2026 it was among the launch brands of TESTED by SuppCo, a program that buys products without notice and has an ISO-accredited lab check that ingredients and doses match the label. SuppCo's own write-up does not name which Metagenics protein powders were tested or publish heavy-metal results.
          </p>

          <p>
            What Metagenics does not have is a sport certification on its proteins. When we searched NSF's Certified for Sport database on 21 November 2026, no Metagenics products appeared. We could not confirm an Informed Sport listing either. Those programs test every lot for banned substances, which matters only if you are drug-tested; for everyone else, the absence is a missing extra rather than a red flag. Our <a href="/best-nsf-certified-protein-powder/">NSF certified protein powder</a> guide lists proteins that carry it.
          </p>

          <p>
            The brand does hold several narrower certifications. Retail listings for Perfect Protein Whey show Non-GMO, Certified Gluten-Free and Certified B Corporation claims, and Metagenics announced Non-GMO Project Verification for selected products in 2018. Those confirm sourcing and allergen handling, not purity or label accuracy, so do not read them as a substitute for contaminant testing.
          </p>

          <p>
            On heavy metals, Metagenics says it tests for lead, arsenic, cadmium and mercury, but it does not publish per-product results for consumers. One Fullscript listing for UltraMeal Advanced Protein carries a California Prop 65 lead warning, which is common for plant-protein and cocoa-containing powders and means the daily dose exceeds California's very strict threshold, not a federal safety limit. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-heavy-metals/">protein powder without heavy metals</a> guide covers brands that publish numbers.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is also some regulatory history worth knowing. In 2013 the FDA sent Metagenics a warning letter saying several products, including UltraMeal Plus and UltraMeal Plus 360, were misbranded as medical foods and that website claims made them unapproved drugs, as summarised by ConsumerLab. That concerned marketing claims, not contamination, and it is more than a decade old. It is still a reason to read Metagenics' health claims with the same scepticism you would apply to any supplement brand.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="lineup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Which Metagenics Protein Powders Are Sold Now?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Metagenics currently sells protein in three families: Perfect Protein, BioPure and UltraMeal. Perfect Protein Whey and Perfect Protein Pea and Rice are straightforward 20 g protein powders. BioPure Protein is an unflavored whey protein concentrate marketed for its naturally occurring immunoglobulins. The UltraMeal family covers meal shakes and medical foods, of which UltraMeal Advanced Protein is the clearest protein product.
          </p>

          <p>
            Names have shifted, which confuses buyers. UltraMeal Rice is now sold as Essential Nutrition Shake on several dispensaries, described as formerly UltraMeal Rice. UltraMeal Plus 360 and UltraMeal Cardio 360 still appear in some practitioner catalogs, but we could not confirm current consumer pricing or labels for them. We found no current product sold under the name PaleoGenics, so if a practitioner mentions it, ask which product they mean.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where you buy matters. Metagenics is a practitioner-channel brand: most products are sold through doctors, chiropractors and nutritionists, or through dispensaries such as Fullscript that require a practitioner link. Some products, including Perfect Protein Whey and Pea and Rice, are now also listed by Vitacost and Walmart. The brand's policies have long objected to unauthorized third-party marketplace sellers, so treat cheap listings from unknown Amazon or eBay sellers with caution, as storage and expiry cannot be checked.
          </p>

          <p>
            Metagenics' own website blocked our requests, and many dispensaries hide prices until you sign in, so the prices in this review come from retail listings that showed them publicly on 21 November 2026. Your practitioner may charge more or less. Ask for the per-serving price and do the per-gram arithmetic below before you commit to a monthly order.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Which Metagenics Protein Powders Are Worth Buying?
          </h2>

          <p>
            These are the five Metagenics protein and shake powders we could confirm are on sale through retailers and practitioner dispensaries in November 2026. Prices are retailer prices we found on 21 November 2026 (Metagenics does not publish consumer prices on most practitioner listings); nutrition figures are from retailer copies of the label and can vary by flavor.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What Is in Metagenics Perfect Protein Whey?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Perfect Protein Whey is a whey isolate and hydrolyzed whey isolate blend with 20 g of protein in a 27 g, two-scoop serving, per the Natural Vanilla label on Vitacost. That is 74 percent protein by weight, which is good. The serving has 100 calories, 1.5 g of fat, 2 g of carbohydrate, 1 g of added sugar and 200 mg of sodium. The amino acid profile lists about 2.1 g of leucine per serving.
          </p>

          <p>
            The ingredient list is short and readable: whey protein isolate, hydrolyzed whey protein isolate, medium-chain triglycerides, organic cane sugar, gum acacia, silica, salt, rice bran, natural flavors and rebaudioside A from stevia. There are no artificial sweeteners or colors. The hydrolyzed fraction is pre-digested and may suit people who find concentrate heavy, a topic our <a href="/best-hydrolyzed-whey-protein/">hydrolyzed whey protein</a> guide covers.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
