                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">12 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fructose, rice syrup solids</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The rest of the range is less impressive on paper. BioPure Protein is only about 70 percent protein, 16 g in a 23 g serving, because it is a concentrate; it contains whey protein concentrate and soy lecithin, so it is not soy-free. The Essential Nutrition Shake gives just 12 g of rice protein with 12 g of sugars from fructose and rice syrup solids in 160 calories, which makes it a meal base, not a protein powder.
          </p>

          <p>
            Allergens follow the protein source: the whey products and BioPure contain milk, BioPure also lists soy, and the pea and rice products are dairy-free. Perfect Protein Pea and Rice is marketed with 3.5 g of added essential amino acids, and UltraMeal Advanced Protein adds leucine and branched-chain amino acids. Added aminos can lift a protein figure, so check whether your label counts them inside or outside the protein total.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="medical-food" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. What Is a Medical Food, and Do You Need UltraMeal?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most people do not need UltraMeal Advanced Protein. Metagenics labels it a medical food for the nutritional management of sarcopenia, the age-related loss of muscle, to be used under the supervision of a physician or licensed practitioner. Medical food is an FDA category for products used under medical supervision for a disease with distinctive nutritional needs, and it is not the same as an approved drug.
          </p>

          <p>
            On the label, UltraMeal Advanced Protein is a pea and rice protein shake with added amino acids, including leucine, plus fiber, olive oil and, in some flavors, cocoa polyphenols. The Dutch chocolate version lists 17 g of protein in a 42 g serving with 14 servings per tub; listings put the plain version higher. A French vanilla listing shows 8 g of fiber, about 1 g of sugar and monk fruit as the sweetener.
          </p>

          <p>
            The idea behind it is reasonable. Older adults respond less to protein, and extra leucine can help a plant protein act more like whey. But a leucine-rich whey isolate does the same job for a fraction of the price, and it is the protein used in much of the sarcopenia research. If you are an older adult trying to protect muscle, our guide to the <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-seniors/">best protein powder for seniors</a> covers cheaper complete proteins.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your doctor has prescribed UltraMeal as part of a supervised plan, follow that plan and ask them before switching. The point here is narrower: there is nothing in the label that an ordinary consumer cannot get from a cheaper complete protein, and the medical-food designation describes how the product is meant to be used, not that it has been proven superior. Health caveat: this review is not medical advice.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost-per-25g" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
